<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>The desire for happiness</h4>
<p>This is a universal theme that connects with everyone. We all seek happiness, but often in wrong places. Help participants identify their &laquo;false promises of happiness&raquo; without judging them, simply acknowledging them. Salt water is a good metaphor: it gives thirst instead of quenching it.</p>

<h4>The beatitudes as paradox</h4>
<p>The beatitudes are countercultural. Jesus did not come to confirm our way of thinking, but to transform it. Help the group feel the force of this paradox: the world says one thing, Jesus says another. It is not about being poor or suffering for its own sake, but about trusting that God has the last word.</p>

<h4>Not ethics, but the Kingdom</h4>
<p>Present the beatitudes not as another ethical code, but as the manifestation of the Kingdom of God. The Kingdom is already present, though not in its fullness. Living the beatitudes is anticipating heaven.</p>

<h4>Eternal beatitude</h4>
<p>Heaven is not a &laquo;prize&raquo; for good people, but the destiny for which we were created. Help participants to have hope and to see the present life as a path toward that goal.</p>

<h4>Sensitivity with suffering</h4>
<p>Some participants may be confused by &laquo;blessed are those who mourn&raquo; or &laquo;blessed are the persecuted.&raquo; Clarify that suffering is not good in itself, but that God can transform it and give it meaning. It is not a call to pain, but to trust.</p>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>&#x1F331; Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use the image of a hidden treasure: the beatitudes are the treasure Jesus shows us. Activity: draw their favorite beatitude. Memorize with gestures &laquo;Blessed are the pure in heart.&raquo;</p>

<h4>&#x1F33F; Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect with their search for happiness in social media, success, popularity. The beatitudes are a radical alternative path. Ask: &laquo;Have you ever felt that success does not give the happiness it promised?&raquo;</p>

<h4>&#x1FAB4; Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults have experienced the frustration of placing their happiness in things that do not last. The beatitudes offer real hope. Help them examine where they have placed their trust and redirect it toward God.</p>

<h4>&#x1F333; Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the theology of beatitude: the beatific vision in St. Thomas Aquinas, the lumen gloriae, the relationship between grace and glory. Discuss how the beatitudes are the ethics of the Kingdom that anticipates eternal life.</p>
</section>

