<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>&laquo;Does this mean Christians should not be happy in this life?&raquo;</h4>
<p>No. The beatitudes do not deny earthly happiness, but reorient it. God wants our happiness, but an authentic happiness that does not depend on external circumstances. The saints are the happiest people who have ever existed: their joy came not from having everything, but from having God.</p>

<h4>&laquo;Are the poor automatically blessed?&raquo;</h4>
<p>No. Material poverty is not automatically a beatitude. Jesus says &laquo;poor in spirit&raquo;: those who recognize their need for God, who do not trust in their own strength but in God. A rich person can be &laquo;poor in spirit&raquo; if they live with humility and detachment. A poor person may not be if they are bitter or envious.</p>

<h4>&laquo;What about those who suffer unjustly? Are they blessed even if they do not believe?&raquo;</h4>
<p>The beatitudes are promises of the Kingdom for those who follow Christ. But God does not abandon anyone who suffers. His mercy extends to all. The beatitude of those who mourn is a promise of comfort for all who suffer, though perhaps only believers can fully understand its meaning.</p>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus, Master of the beatitudes,<br />
teach me to live as you live.<br />
Give me a poor and humble heart,<br />
meek and merciful,<br />
pure and peaceful.<br />
May my life be an announcement of your Kingdom,<br />
and may I help others discover<br />
that true happiness is found in you.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>
</section>

